To find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array using C++, we can use a different approach than the one used in the given Java code snippet.
Here's a C++ function that finds the second largest integer element in an unsorted array:
```
int second_largest(int arr[], int n)
{
    // Initialize two variables to store the maximum and second maximum elements
    int max1 = INT_MIN;
    int max2 = INT_MIN;

    // Iterate through each element in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        // If current element is greater than max1, update max1 and max2
        if (arr[i] > max1)
        {
            max2 = max1;
            max1 = arr[i];
        }
        // Otherwise, if current element is between max1 and max2, update max2
        else if (arr[i] > max2 && arr[i] != max1)
        {
            max2 = arr[i];
        }
    }

    // Return the second largest element
    return max2;
}
```
This function works as follows:
- We initialize two variables `max1` and `max2` to store the maximum and second maximum elements, respectively. These variables are initialized with the minimum possible value (`INT_MIN`) so that any element in the array will be larger than these values initially.
- We iterate through each element in the array and compare it with `max1` and `max2`. If the current element is greater than `max1`, we update both `max1` and `max2`. If the current element is not equal to `max1`, but is still greater than `max2`, we update only `max2`.
- After iterating through the entire array, we return the value stored in `max2`, which represents the second largest element.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the length of the array. This is because we iterate through the array once to populate `max1` and `max2`. Therefore, this function meets the requirement of having a time complexity of O(n) or less.
